## translate() vs Absolute Positioning

## The Rendering Pipeline Difference

When you animate `top` or `left`:
1. **Layout** — recalculates geometry for affected elements (expensive)
2. **Paint** — redraws pixels
3. **Composite** — assembles layers

When you animate `transform: translate()`:
1. ~~Layout~~ — skipped
2. ~~Paint~~ — skipped
3. **Composite only** — GPU moves the existing layer

## Performance Impact

```css
/* Expensive — triggers layout + paint on every frame */
@keyframes slide-bad {
  from { left: 0; }
  to   { left: 100px; }
}

/* Cheap — compositor only, smooth 60fps */
@keyframes slide-good {
  from { transform: translateX(0); }
  to   { transform: translateX(100px); }
}
```

## Absolute Positioning Use Cases

```css
/* Static placement — no animation, no problem */
.tooltip {
  position: absolute;
  top: 100%;
  left: 0;
}

.badge {
  position: absolute;
  top: -4px;
  right: -4px;
}
```

## Combining Both

A common pattern: use `absolute` for placement, `translate` for micro-animation:

```css
.dropdown {
  position: absolute;
  top: 100%;
  transform: translateY(-4px);
  opacity: 0;
  transition: transform 0.15s, opacity 0.15s;
}

.dropdown.open {
  transform: translateY(0);
  opacity: 1;
}
```

## will-change

Hint the browser to promote an element to its own compositor layer before animation begins:

```css
.animated { will-change: transform; }
/* Remove after animation — layers consume GPU memory */
```

