## Visually Hiding Content for Screen Readers

## The Problem

Some content is visual-only (icon buttons, decorative images) or screen-reader-only (skip links, extra context). The hiding technique must be chosen carefully based on whether assistive technology should still access it.

## Comparison Table

| Technique | Visible | Screen reader | Use case |
|---|---|---|---|
| `display: none` | No | No | Fully hide from everyone |
| `visibility: hidden` | No | No | Hide but preserve layout space |
| `opacity: 0` | No | Yes | Animate in/out |
| `sr-only` class | No | **Yes** | Screen-reader-only text |
| `aria-hidden="true"` | Yes | No | Decorative icon |

## The sr-only Pattern

```css
.sr-only {
  position: absolute;
  width: 1px;
  height: 1px;
  padding: 0;
  margin: -1px;
  overflow: hidden;
  clip-path: inset(50%);
  white-space: nowrap;
  border: 0;
}
```

## Focusable sr-only (Skip Links)

Skip links should become visible on focus so keyboard users can see them:

```css
.skip-link {
  position: absolute;
  top: -100%;
  left: 0;
}

.skip-link:focus {
  top: 0;
  z-index: 9999;
  padding: 0.5rem 1rem;
  background: #000;
  color: #fff;
}
```

## Icon Button Example

```html
<button aria-label="Close">
  <svg aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">...</svg>
  <span class="sr-only">Close</span>
</button>
```

