## Responsive vs Mobile-First

## Responsive Design

The layout adapts to any screen size. The key tools are:
- **Fluid grids** — `%`, `fr`, `auto-fill`
- **Flexible images** — `max-width: 100%`
- **Media queries** — apply different styles at breakpoints

## Desktop-First (max-width queries)

```css
/* Base: desktop layout */
.grid { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr 1fr; }

/* Override for smaller screens */
@media (max-width: 768px) {
  .grid { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
}
```

Small devices download and parse **all** the desktop CSS first, then override it.

## Mobile-First (min-width queries)

```css
/* Base: mobile layout */
.grid { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr; }

/* Progressive enhancement for larger screens */
@media (min-width: 640px)  { .grid { grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; } }
@media (min-width: 1024px) { .grid { grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr 1fr; } }
```

**Advantages of mobile-first:**
- Smaller devices download less CSS (no overrides to parse).
- Forces you to prioritise essential content — everything added on larger screens is an enhancement.
- Aligns with how most users actually browse (>60% mobile traffic globally).

## Modern Enhancement: Container Queries

```css
@container (min-width: 400px) {
  .card { flex-direction: row; }
}
```

Container queries respond to the **component's container size**, not the viewport — enabling truly portable responsive components.

