## box-sizing: border-box

## The Default Problem (content-box)

```css
.box {
  width: 200px;
  padding: 20px;
  border: 2px solid black;
}
/* Rendered width = 200 + 40 (padding) + 4 (border) = 244px */
/* Breaks percentage-based layouts */
```

## border-box Solution

```css
.box {
  box-sizing: border-box;
  width: 200px;
  padding: 20px;
  border: 2px solid black;
}
/* Rendered width = exactly 200px */
/* Content area = 200 - 40 - 4 = 156px */
```

## Global Application

```css
/* Recommended reset */
*, *::before, *::after {
  box-sizing: border-box;
}
```

Applying via `*` (universal selector) is cheap — the browser calculates it once. Using inheritance is slightly more performant in theory but rarely matters in practice.

## Why It Matters

```css
/* Two columns, side by side — works perfectly with border-box */
.col {
  box-sizing: border-box;
  width: 50%;
  padding: 1rem;
  float: left;
}
/* With content-box: 50% + padding overflows to 100%+ */
```

## Tailwind and Frameworks

Tailwind CSS includes `box-sizing: border-box` in its preflight. Next.js global styles and most CSS resets set it globally. You rarely need to think about it if you're starting a modern project.

