## SCSS vs Sass Syntax

## Side-by-Side Comparison

The same styles in both syntaxes:

```sass
// .sass — indented (whitespace-significant)
$primary: #0070f3

.button
  background: $primary
  padding: 0.5rem 1rem

  &:hover
    background: darken($primary, 10%)

  &--large
    font-size: 1.25rem
```

```scss
// .scss — superset of CSS
$primary: #0070f3;

.button {
  background: $primary;
  padding: 0.5rem 1rem;

  &:hover {
    background: darken($primary, 10%);
  }

  &--large {
    font-size: 1.25rem;
  }
}
```

## Key Differences

| | .sass | .scss |
|---|---|---|
| Braces | No `{}` | Required |
| Semicolons | No `;` | Required |
| CSS compatibility | Any valid CSS must be rewritten | Any valid CSS is valid SCSS |
| Adoption | Niche / older projects | Dominant, all major tooling |

## Why SCSS Won

- **Zero migration cost** — drop a `.css` file in, rename to `.scss`, done.
- **Familiar syntax** — any CSS developer can read it immediately.
- **Tooling** — webpack, Vite, PostCSS, and editors all support `.scss` natively.

## Sass Modules (Modern)

Both syntaxes now use `@use` and `@forward` instead of the deprecated `@import`:

```scss
@use 'sass:color';
@use 'tokens' as t;

.button { background: color.adjust(t.$primary, $lightness: -10%); }
```

